A||||8||JohnnyK||On a different note:  Networking 2 computers||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-13-2001||04:24 PM||||Curious about just doing this for gaming. I used to do it with null modem cables, but if I disconnect the cable modem, and plug in a network cable that goes to the other computer (looks like it also has a network card), can I connect them like that using the IPX (or whatever it's called) option in the games? How would i find the addresses and what not? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||09-13-2001||04:34 PM||||Just connect both computers through the network cards, goto Control Panel -&gt; Network, choose "Add", choose "Client", and install "Client for Microsoft Networking". It'll install the IPX/SPX, NetBeui, and TCP/IP for your network card (if you want, you can delete the IPX/SPX and Netbeui protocols). Restart both computers.<P>After you have restarted the computers, go to Start -&gt; Run, and type: winipcfg. A window will open with the ip-adress of your machine (usually something like 192.168.0.x). Most multiplayer games support TCP/IP play. Start a game in "Server" mode on one of the machines and on the other machine, connect to that game using the "server"'s ip-address.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||09-13-2001||04:47 PM||||But then I assume I couldn't just plug the cable modem back in and everything would be all right. I'd have to do that with the cable modem too, each time I switched them back and forth, right?||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||09-13-2001||04:53 PM||||Another option is to buy a network hub and set up the cable modem and both computers to all connect with that, it's what I use, works well. With directly connecting 2 systems together you need to use a crossover cable. (any telco or isp can make you one.)<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||09-13-2001||05:22 PM||||Buy a Linksys Cable/DSL router and plug in your cable and your two computers.  Each computer needs an Ethernet card.  And this is assuming that your cable comes into a "Cable Modem" which then connects to your computer via and Ethernet cable.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||09-13-2001||05:28 PM||||Ah, I read your message to fast. I overlooked the fact that you have a cable modem also. There are a few things you can do. You can, as DarkLord suggested, buy a hub and connect everything to that. Problem might be that some conflicts may arise since the cable modem might "learn" the wrong Mac address. This depends on what kind of cable modem you have, you might want to ask your provider about this. Hubs are, however, dirt cheap (I got myself a SohoWare 8-port 10/100Mbit auto-sensing hub at Best Buy for $39).<P>Another thing you can do is get a Cable Modem <B>router</B>. This will work for sure but is a bit (actually a lot) more expensive.<P>Third thing you can do, is put a second network card in your computer that has the cable modem connected. You'll connect both computers using this network card, and use the original network card for your cable modem. Install Internet Connection Sharing and you are also able to get on the internet from the second computer. Disadvantage is, that if you want to connect a third computer, you'll still need a HUB or a router.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||09-13-2001||05:40 PM||||If you don't use a hub (or switch), you need to use a crossover cable between the two machines. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||LarryB||09-13-2001||05:55 PM||||The other possibility is if your provider has what is referred to as a home-sharing option; they provision your cable modem to accept more than 1 NIC card. If so each machine will be assigned its own IP address directly on the external network; you only need to buy a cheap hub to connect to the cable modem and then to each of your machines.<P>MediaOne (my provider at home) offers this option; for $10/month you can connect 2 additional machines simultaneously.<P>Just remember if you use any kind of software based solution with just the 2 machines you will need to install a 2nd NIC card in the 1 machine you are connecting to your cable modem.   You should be able to buy a crossover cable at a better PC supply house or even an electrical supply house that also sells telecom equipment.  Should run you no more than around $10-15 CDN. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||09-13-2001||06:15 PM||||I work for a cablemodem ISP... Here's what you can do to make it Plug-n-play:<P>1) You can go straight-thru x-over cable, adn leave your settings as-is with a few minor changes. If you are going that route, make sure that File and Print sharing is enabled on both. You don't need to use IPX/SPX networking. Just use TCP/ip. <P>2) Leave computer name alone. For the @home network (media1, roadrunner, at&T, Cox, Shaw, Videon, etc) the modem ignores the MAC address on the computer. Rather, the DHCP server polls off the computer name/DNS name. <BR>The only change you SHOULD make"<BR>-SPECIFY an IP address of 10.0.0.XX for both machines, making them different. MAKE SURE THAT BOTH ARE ON THE SAME SUBNET!<P>3) When you're done, just disable File And Print Sharing on the network card, and set the TCP/IP for the NIC to "Obtain Automatically." If it gives you any hassle, just reboot machine, powercycle modem.||||reg||7
